    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d967">

                <pb facs="0855" n="739" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg739"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 312.115D69.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the American Consul at Juárez</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-03-07">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 7, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Department has received from the Dolores Mines Co., El Paso, a request<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d967-f1"/> that you take charge $51,000 worth silver and gold
                    bullion <pb facs="0856" n="740" xml:id="pg740"/> and harbor in consulate until
                    it can be legally exported. They have been informed that under existing
                    conditions Department can not authorize or permit you to receive on deposit
                    bullion or money.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Huntington Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
